# 06. BACKGROUND MOTION AND GRAPHIC ART DIRECTION

Build the background and artwork as a coherent design system.

Avoid generic animated blobs on every section.

Use a combination of:
- Saturated gradient fields.
- Abstract SVG silhouettes.
- Layered geometric shapes.
- Fine curved lines.
- Subtle grain where performant.
- Soft color transitions.
- Selective 3D-style composition.
- Tiny animated accent elements.

Motion should evoke ideas drifting, content assembling, and creative momentum.

The hero artwork may use a few separate animation layers with slightly different durations.

Some shapes can slowly float. A line may trace a path. A graphic object may gently rotate a few degrees. Cards may enter their composition with a restrained stagger.

Every effect should have a clear visual purpose.

## Motion engineering

- Prefer CSS transforms and opacity for animation.
- Avoid animating layout-heavy properties.
- Do not use a large continuously running canvas when SVG or CSS will suffice.
- Avoid continuously updating React state for background movement.
- Avoid scroll listeners that perform expensive work on every event.
- Use IntersectionObserver for appropriate section reveals.
- Use scroll-linked animations only when supported and genuinely beneficial.
- Provide a reduced-motion experience.
- Pause nonessential animation when the document is hidden where practical.
- Avoid sudden changes in artwork dimensions.
- Make decorative elements ignore pointer input.
- Keep content readable even while graphics move.

Use a restrained range of animation durations. Micro-interactions should feel crisp; larger visual transitions can be slightly slower.

Do not force users to scroll through a predetermined animation. Their scroll position must always remain under their control.

# 07. THE ACTUAL WORKING PRODUCT

The landing page is the entrance. The content studio is the product.

Create a coherent, responsive application experience.

## A. Dashboard

Provide:
- A clear greeting.
- A prominent "New content plan" action.
- Recent campaigns.
- Actual saved draft counts.
- Drafts awaiting review.
- Upcoming locally scheduled content.
- A preview of the content board.
- Useful first-time empty states.

Use real local application data rather than fake statistics.

## B. Campaign creation

Create a polished, easy-to-complete campaign form.

Fields:
- Campaign name.
- Initial idea or brief.
- Target audience.
- Content objective.
- Preferred tone.
- Language.
- Target platforms.
- Number of requested drafts.
- Optional keywords.
- Optional call to action.
- Optional writing constraints.

Include well-considered defaults.

The user must be able to complete the basic workflow without filling out every optional field.

Provide validation, accessible labels, inline errors, and loading states.

Preserve all entered information when returning to a previous step or recovering from an inference error.

## C. Open-weight AI content generation

Open-weight inference is the primary intelligence behind the product.

The model should transform the user's campaign brief into structured, relevant, platform-specific drafts.

Each draft may contain:
- Platform.
- Suggested hook or title.
- Main text.
- Suggested hashtags, when relevant.
- Call to action, when appropriate.
- A short explanation of the content angle, where useful.

Include actions for:
- Generate drafts.
- Regenerate a single draft.
- Generate alternative hooks.
- Make the text shorter.
- Change the tone.
- Repurpose to another platform.
- Edit inline.
- Save.
- Copy.
- Export.

All generated drafts must be editable.

Validate structured model output before rendering it. Handle malformed output, incomplete responses, model-loading failures, inference timeouts, and unsupported device capabilities without losing existing work.

Never render untrusted model output as executable HTML.

## D. Model architecture

Investigate browser-compatible open-weight models before choosing one.

Preferred architecture:
- React and TypeScript frontend.
- A model adapter separating inference from the UI.
- Hugging Face Transformers.js with WebGPU when supported by the selected model.
- A compatible quantized checkpoint where practical.
- An appropriate fallback, such as CPU inference or a separately configured local inference server.

Choose a model whose format, architecture, license, and inference requirements have been verified.

Do not simply select a popular model name without checking whether it is compatible with the chosen runtime.

Keep prompt construction, model initialization, inference, response parsing, and application state in separate modules.

Load the model lazily. Do not prevent users from exploring the landing page or workspace while it initializes.

Display the actual model name and inference mode.

Clearly distinguish:
- Loading model.
- Model ready.
- Generating.
- Local inference unavailable.
- Demo mode.
- Generation failed.

If using local browser inference, disclose that initial model downloads may require internet access. Offline operation should only be claimed when the required model assets and application resources are available locally.

If a remote endpoint is used, disclose that the submitted content is sent to that endpoint.

Never hardcode private API credentials into frontend code.

Primary reference:
https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers.js/en/guides/webgpu

