# 09. RESPONSIVE QUALITY

The website must be fully responsive, not just technically narrow.

Test at:
- 320 px.
- 375 px.
- 430 px.
- 768 px.
- 1024 px.
- 1440 px and wider.

On small screens:
- Stack hero content naturally.
- Scale abstract artwork to fit without clipping important elements.
- Avoid horizontal scrolling.
- Use compact navigation.
- Keep form controls touch-friendly.
- Adapt the content board and calendar.
- Avoid fixed-width desktop layouts.
- Make dialogs fit the available viewport.
- Preserve readable type sizes.
- Ensure no interaction depends exclusively on hover.

Use roughly 44 × 44 CSS-pixel touch targets for primary controls wherever practical.

# 10. SCROLLING — THE NON-NEGOTIABLE STANDARD

Users must be able to scroll smoothly from top to bottom and back to the top without layout jumps, lag, trapped scrolling, disappearing content, or broken interactions.

Implement the following:

- Preserve native browser scroll behavior.
- Do not hijack wheel or touch events for decorative motion.
- Do not globally disable touch gestures.
- Avoid scroll-jacking libraries.
- Do not use unnecessary full-page snap behavior.
- Ensure fixed and sticky headers never hide section headings.
- Keep decorative motion independent of document scrolling.
- Do not force a scroll position after a section animation.
- Avoid layout shifts from loading fonts, images, illustrations, and model-related interface elements.
- Ensure all section transitions work in both directions.
- Keep nested scroll containers limited to places that genuinely need them.
- Avoid adding high-cost blur effects to enormous moving layers.
- Ensure dialogs and mobile menus scroll correctly when their content exceeds the viewport.
- Avoid horizontal overflow across all breakpoints.

Use stable dimensions and aspect ratios for visual assets.

If IntersectionObserver reveals content, ensure content remains discoverable and accessible when JavaScript animation is unavailable. Do not make important content permanently invisible because an observer failed.

Any motion that creates visible jitter, delays interaction, or causes frame drops must be simplified.

# 11. PREMIUM MICRO-INTERACTIONS

Every interaction should feel deliberate.

Buttons should have a restrained change in color or depth when pressed.

Cards may lift slightly on desktop hover without moving the underlying layout.

Tabs should transition cleanly between states.

Dropdowns should appear quickly and close predictably.

Section reveals may use gentle opacity and transform transitions.

The hero artwork should create a feeling of life, not chaos.

Use animation to improve comprehension and perceived quality, never to distract from task completion.

Support prefers-reduced-motion throughout the app.

